      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended here. This curve, f of x equals x cubed minus three x, has a local maximum at minus one, two. Picture it as a stretch of roller coaster track, with f the height in metres above the platform. The stretch runs from x equals minus two to x equals three, with both ends included. Near minus one there's a small hump, and nothing nearby is higher than two. But ride on to the end of the stretch, up the big climb. At x equals three, f is eighteen. So a local maximum isn't always the largest value. How do we find the largest and smallest values on a whole interval, and make sure we haven't missed one?"
    },
